  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nMoisture advection increases Thursday night as an initial \nshortwave trough ejects from northern Mexico into the Southern \nPlains. The NBM brings a sharp gradient of PoPs (20-80%), mostly \nconfined to the northwest quadrant of the CWA by early Friday \nmorning. Consensus in model data have slowed the eastern progress \nof the rain, keeping the majority of far eastern Oklahoma and \nnorthwest Arkansas practically rain-free through much of the day \nFriday. Rain chances will gradually become more widespread and are\nstill expected to peak overnight Friday night into Saturday \nafternoon (PoPs 40-60%) as synoptic lift spreads over the CWA \nahead of the slowly advancing mid-level wave. As the shortwave \ntrough axis finally passes east of the area Saturday afternoon, \nrain chances will steadily end west-to-east. Ensemble and \ndeterministic guidance both show very limited instability, which \nshould keep thunder chances low through this particular storm \nevent. Rainfall totals will stay generally light, ranging from a \nfew hundredths of an inch to around a quarter of an inch. However,\na few isolated areas may have slightly higher totals, closer to\nhalf an inch, especially north of I-44 and west of Highway 75 in \nnortheast Oklahoma. \n\nA brief break in rain chances looks probable on Sunday as the\nshortwave lifts northeast toward the Great Lakes, resulting in dry\nand mild weather. Focus then turns to a significantly deeper\ntrough/cut-off low that will dig across the western and central\nCONUS Monday through Wednesday. Overall, there is good agreement\nin models in showing impactful weather resulting from this system.\nGlobal deterministic models alongside AI/machine-learning suites\ndepict strong run-to-run consistency on a potent cold front\npushing through the Southern Plains, but continue to differ on \nthe exact timing on its passage. While timing and spatial details\nstill carry high ensemble spread, higher rain and storm chances \n(NBM PoPs 50-70%) are expected to precede the boundary on Monday \nand Tuesday, followed by a noticeable post-frontal cool down \ntoward more seasonal values (highs in the 60s, lows in the 40s) \nby next Wednesday. Continue to check back for updates as forecast \nspecifics are refined.\n\nMejia",
